{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades a powerful military leader and a shrewd politician navigating the complex relationships between Christian and Muslim powers in the Levant. His reign was marked by both military successes and diplomatic challenges solidifying Tripoli's position as a major Crusader state.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond III was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Hauteville a daughter of Bohemond II Duke of Antioch. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1154. His early reign faced immediate threats from Muslim forces particularly the Zengi dynasty of Mosul and Aleppo. He skillfully defended Tripoli against numerous sieges and raids maintaining its independence through a combination of military strength and strategic alliances. He strengthened the county's defenses rebuilding fortifications and expanding its military capabilities.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw extensive military campaigns against the Zengids and other Muslim rulers. He engaged in numerous battles often achieving tactical victories though a decisive defeat was rare. He formed alliances with other Crusader states like Antioch and Jerusalem to counter the growing power of Zengi. His military prowess earned him a reputation as a formidable warrior respected by both Christian and Muslim adversaries. He also engaged in diplomatic efforts attempting to secure truces and maintain peaceful relations when possible.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances* A key aspect of Raymond III's rule was his skillful diplomacy. He understood that Tripoli could not survive in isolation and actively sought alliances with other Crusader states and even with Muslim rulers when it served his interests. He maintained a complex web of relationships balancing cooperation and conflict. He engaged in negotiations with Nur ad-Din Zengi and later with Saladin seeking to secure favorable terms for Tripoli. His diplomatic efforts helped to stabilize the region and protect Tripoli's interests. He often used marriage alliances to strengthen his position further securing beneficial partnerships with influential families.\n\n*Later Years and Legacy* In his later years Raymond III faced increasing pressure from Saladin who consolidated Muslim power in the Levant. He continued to defend Tripoli valiantly but ultimately could not prevent its eventual fall to Saladin in 1183. Despite the loss of Tripoli Raymond III left a lasting legacy as a capable ruler a skilled military leader and a shrewd diplomat. He is remembered as one of the most important Counts of Tripoli successfully defending his county for nearly fifty years. His reign represents a high point in the history of the Crusader states demonstrating the resilience and adaptability of the Crusader presence in the Holy Land. He is a significant figure in the history of the Crusades often portrayed as a pragmatic leader focused on the survival and prosperity of his county.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -3.099436753473128e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.29277363419532776}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.39782649278640747}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.15701399743556976}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.0002329078270122409}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.0265373677830212e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.271740287251305e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -9.881961887003854e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.004122094251215458}, {"token": "▁until", "logprob": 